PROMPT:

Build me a playable MVP of a 3D arcade wrestling game in Three.js with the grappling depth,
reversal drama, and physicality of the golden era of console wrestling.

Everything original: original wrestlers, names, finishers, arena, presentation, audio, visual
identity. No copyrighted characters, likenesses, logos, or music.

START FROM THE EXISTING CODEBASE

Do not start from an empty folder. rork-medieval-3d-chess in this folder is King's Gambit, an
MIT-licensed Vite + React + Three.js 3D chess game with a working renderer, quality presets,
post-processing, procedural textures, synthesised audio, a skeletal animation layer, a throttled
GLB loader, a HUD, and Playwright browser tests already configured. It also ships 129 GLB models
including 18 rigged humanoid characters with roughly 110 animation clips.

PORTING_PLAN.md is the audit of that repo — what to keep, what to rework, what to delete, and the
order to do it in. Read it. Fork the project, keep its LICENSE and add attribution, strip the
chess out, and build the wrestling game in the space that clears.

The chess rules are worthless to us and the engine infrastructure is worth a great deal. Do not
rewrite what already works, and do not try to preserve anything in the delete list out of caution.

READ FIRST

MECHANICS_SPEC.md in this folder is the reverse-engineered system design for the engine I want:
the grapple matrix, the position and condition state model, the move slot schema and its MVP
subset, the shared animation library architecture, reversal channels, localised damage,
submissions, the finisher meter and its situational requirements, attributes, the AI personality
block, and match rules. It was extracted from the period strategy guide and community
documentation, both of which are also in this folder.

Read it before you design anything. It is a systems document, not an asset library — take the
architecture and the wrestling vocabulary from it, never names or identity.

SCOPE — this is an MVP, hold this line

Two wrestlers with contrasting archetypes. One arena. Singles match only. Roughly 30–40 moves
each, following the MVP subset in the spec: chosen so every system is exercised at least once,
not to fill all 120 slots.

Systems get full depth. Content gets cut. That trade is the whole point — the game feels right
because of the state machine and the reversal windows, not because of the roster size.

Not in the MVP: additional match types, season mode, create-a-wrestler, online, elaborate
entrances, tag and double-team moves.

THE TWO BARS

Correctness. MECHANICS_SPEC.md. A critic reading the spec and driving the running game must be
able to confirm each system behaves as specified. "It has grapples" is not passing. Four base
grapple states, each holding four distinct authored moves, entered separately, independently
reversible, is passing.

Feel. Real wrestling games. There are reference screenshots in this folder already. Collect more
frames and short clips from the best-feeling wrestling games ever shipped and from stylised games
with elite animation and impact, save them into references/, and treat them as the bar for motion
and presentation. Every critic round on feel is a blind A/B: our clip beside a reference clip,
unlabeled, and the critic picks the better one.

The honest target is to beat the 2003 original on looks while matching it on systems. Not to beat
a modern AAA wrestling game — that is not reachable here and pretending otherwise just lets the
loop declare victory early.

Nothing in references/ ships. It exists for critics only.

ASSETS — read this carefully, a previous attempt failed here

Character meshes and rigs are sourced, not generated. Full sourcing plan and licence terms are in
ASSET_SOURCES.md — read it before acquiring anything.

The text-to-3D pipeline the chess manifest points at is NOT available. Do not plan around it.
Bodies come from MakeHuman, rigged through the Mixamo auto-rigger.

Start with the rigged humanoids already in the chess repo as placeholders so the systems work has
bodies to run against from day one. They are medieval soldiers and they look wrong, which is fine
— nothing before the asset phase depends on them looking right.

Build a body morph layer: per-bone scaling on head, neck, shoulders, chest, arms, forearms, hands,
abdomen, waist, thighs, legs and feet, each with independent width, height and length. This is
exactly how the source game's create-a-wrestler worked, and the community guide in this folder
contains real working morph values you can read as a sanity check on sensible ranges. One base mesh
plus a morph profile per character is how two wrestlers get genuinely different builds without two
sets of modelling work.

Use the morph layer for accurate physique variety, not for stylisation. The two MVP wrestlers are a
super-heavyweight powerhouse and a technical cruiserweight — the weight-class rules only become
visible when the bodies differ sharply. Rig at neutral proportions and apply the morph at runtime;
Mixamo's auto-rigger fails on heavily deformed meshes.

Single-body animation is sourced too. Idles, walks, runs, strikes, staggers, knockdowns, get-ups.
Ten usable clips already exist in the chess repo — anim-idle, anim-combat-stance, anim-knock-down,
anim-step-forward-and-push, anim-kneel-on-one-knee-and-stand and the locomotion set. Beyond those,
use the Quaternius Universal Animation Library (CC0, glTF, Mixamo-compatible rig, and its combat
combos ship as separate hits with separate recoveries, which maps onto the strike string and
reversal windows). Fill remaining gaps from Mixamo.

Two-body grapple motion is procedural and IK-driven. Lock-ups, suplexes, powerbombs, pin
transitions. No library has these and no text-to-3D pipeline will give you two skeletons authored
to match. Define contact points, drive both rigs toward authored key poses, correct root motion so
the bodies actually meet. This is the hard problem and it should absorb most of your iteration.

Build it in the post-mixer slot. The chess codebase already proves the pattern: it lets the
animation mixer drive the body, then re-applies a held pose afterwards each frame because the
mixer otherwise owns the skeleton. Our contact correction lives in that same slot. See
PORTING_PLAN.md.

Follow the shared animation library architecture in the spec: one pool of move animations keyed
by family and variant, wrestlers as assembly manifests selecting from that pool, and only 3–5
genuinely unique animations per character for their signatures and finishers.

Everything else stays code-generated: ring, arena, crowd, HUD, VFX, textures, and all audio via
the Web Audio API.

Only CC0 or explicitly free-for-commercial assets. Keep an ATTRIBUTIONS.md with the source and
licence of every sourced file.

An earlier build of this game required every mesh to be generated in code. It produced
featureless capsule-shaped wrestlers. That is the failure mode to avoid.

GRAPHICS

The reference game's own look, executed with modern technology. It aimed at grounded realism and
only looks dated because of PS2 hardware — low polygon counts, tiny textures, no real-time
shadows, flat lighting. The intent was right. Build the game that team would ship today.

So the reference footage IS the art bar for proportion and tone. Match its physiques and its
sense of scale, then render it properly: PBR materials, real-time shadows, an arena lighting rig,
contact shadows under every foot, and restrained post-processing.

Three things this rules out.

No cartoon exaggeration. Not WWE All Stars, not Fortnite. No oversized hands, no waists tapered to
nothing, no shrunken legs. Real wrestlers are already enormous and accurate anatomy gives you the
imposing silhouette for free — a 320lb powerhouse next to a 210lb cruiserweight reads instantly
without any stylisation.

No cel shading. No toon ramps, no hard colour bands, no ink outlines, no halftone. Surfaces shade
smoothly and continuously the way real materials do.

No photorealism. Skin shaders and pore detail are not reachable here, and a failed attempt reads
as plastic — which is exactly why the period screenshots look the way they do. Accurate proportions
with believable materials and good lighting is the target. Realistic anatomy with cheap materials
is the failure mode.

Where the budget goes: lighting, materials and shadows, not polygon count or texture resolution.
That is where the entire generational gap between the reference and this build actually lives.

Priority order, and work it in this order:

1. Silhouette. A wrestler must be identifiable as a solid black shape. This matters more than
   everything below it combined.
2. Lighting. Arena spotlights, warm key and cool fill, coloured rim lights separating bodies from
   the background, and honest contact shadows under every foot — grounding is what sells weight.
3. Materials. Give skin, trunks, boots, leather, canvas, steel and rope genuinely different
   responses. Material variety does more for perceived quality than polygon count.
4. Post. ACES filmic tone mapping, bloom, subtle ambient occlusion, a light colour grade, gentle
   vignette. Keep it restrained.
5. Geometry density. Last, and least important.

Faces stay simple and slightly abstracted. Identity comes from silhouette, build, attire and
palette, not facial detail — chasing expressive faces at this budget is how characters end up
looking wrong.

The full rendering pass comes after the fight feels right. Tuning materials against unproven
gameplay is wasted work.

CONSTRAINTS

Keep the inherited stack: Vite, React, TypeScript, Three.js, Tailwind, shadcn/ui, Vitest and
Playwright. It already matches what I wanted, so do not migrate it. I run the install and the dev
script and I am playing. Nothing else to configure.

Do not add heavy dependencies. A physics engine for weapons and debris is fine if you need one;
a game framework or a state-management library is not.

60fps at 1080p on a mid-range Windows laptop in Chrome. No frame spikes during finishers, table
breaks, or camera transitions.

The canvas fills the viewport and the HUD is an overlay on top of it. A previous build put the
canvas in normal document flow and it ended up squeezed into a corner with text overflowing
around it.

HOW TO WORK

You are the lead. Design the architecture, then break the goal into the smallest pieces that can
be built and judged independently, and fan out builder subagents. You decide the decomposition.
Keep the fan-out modest — the builder/critic split is what matters, not the size of the fleet.

Build the capture harness before anything else. Playwright is already installed and configured in
the inherited repo, so extend it rather than starting over: it must boot the game, drive a
wrestler into any specific state named in the spec, and capture stills, frame sequences and short
clips from multiple angles. Stills cannot judge this game. Contact, weight, reversals and camera
feel only exist in motion, so the harness must produce motion.

Builders never grade their own work. A critic sees only the real captured output, the spec, and
the reference bar — never the builder's history or self-assessment. Critics report defects by
name: "left foot slides during grapple entry", "hands detach from the shoulders mid-suplex",
"Power and Quick base grapples resolve to the same animation", "wrestlers are featureless
blobs with no readable anatomy", "reversal window is visually unreadable", "landing resolves too
fast to read as weight", "camera hides the pin on impact". A critic that approves work to be
agreeable has failed its job.

Loop each piece: build, capture, judge against both bars, name the biggest remaining gap, send it
back. Cap it at roughly three rounds per piece for this MVP, then move on and log what is still
weak. I would rather have every system at 70 percent than two systems perfect and the rest
missing.

Prove the match is fun before it is pretty. Get the state machine, the move table, reversal
windows and damage tuned while the wrestlers are still untextured. Rendering comes after.

After each wave, have one fresh agent play the whole game end to end and smooth it into one
coherent thing rather than a pile of separately improved parts.

PROGRESS

Maintain a live progress page I can open and refresh from my phone. Show what each subagent is
working on, the latest clips beside their reference comparisons, which spec systems are verified
and which are not, and the current biggest gaps. Keep it current so I never have to interrupt you
to ask how it is going.

Pause after the capture harness and the first playable grapple exchange so I can look before you
commit to the whole pipeline.

REPORTING

Tell me the truth about quality. If the grapple system is at 60 percent, if the rig is producing
foot sliding, if impacts feel weak, say so and say why. I would rather hear it from you than find
it myself.

Use subagents.
